A||||13||difranco||exhaust manifold glowing cherry hot
Z||000000||difranco||08-26-99||09:09 PM||||just replaced the distributer and fuel pump and pulsator,  now when it is running the exhaust manifold is glowing cherry red like a piece of charcoal.   PLEASE HELP......
Z||000001||BOSTITCH||08-26-99||09:22 PM||||I've seen that before I believe thats why they caught on fire (in combination with the suposed heat resistant pading or whatever it is) So I went and bought a pair of free flow headers but you could probably make the stocks a bit better by grinding some $#!+ out of there.<p>TITCH<br>
Z||000002||JetroGT||08-26-99||09:50 PM||||It could be a clogged Catilitic Converter. I had that replaced on my old 2M4. After that the manifold stoped getting cracked.
Z||000003||Kage86GT4SP||08-27-99||12:56 AM||||My vote's for the cat.  The only other time I saw a red manifold was on a Saab Turbo.. and that was direcly below the turbo.  Scared the hell out of me.<p>K.
Z||000004||DXR_DAD||08-27-99||01:09 AM||||The best thing I did for my fiero was re-weld and port the exhaust manifolds (I got new ones). <br>Then I re-did the whole exhaust system, new free-flow cat, new 2 1/4" pipe, muffler, and tips, the car runs better than new??? and sounds  MONSTER I mean real good. Every fiero owner should do this.<p>PS. I got all my info on this mod from this fourm!!<p>GOOD LUCK
Z||000005||mwbackus||08-27-99||03:51 AM||||In my experience, this is usually caused by a lean mixture, timing problem, or extreme misfire.  The following issues will cause this condition:<p>1)  Vacuum leak<p>2)  Incorrect timing<p>3)  Spark plug wire routed wrong<p>Of course any other problem which causes a lean mixture will also cause this.  It is not advisable to drive with this condition as you stand a good chance of frying a piston among other things.<p>Mike
Z||000006||Pretender||08-27-99||04:17 AM||||Once in a while mine glows cherry too, it can't be the cat because i don't have one. i'd have to say it's because of running lean too, also it only happens when i first start the car. I'm not to worried about it, i plan on upgrading to a set of Sprint headers..
Z||000007||toopak||08-27-99||07:55 AM||||STOP, CEASE & DESIST!!!!  DON'T RUN THE ENGINE AGAIN UNTIL YOU HAVE A TIMING LIGHT HOOKED UP AND READY TO CHECK TIMING.   The same thing happened to me after a rebuild  & I would bet your timing is off since you had dist. out
Z||000008||terryk||08-27-99||10:21 PM||||Check the fuel pressure and check the timing. It's either lean or way out in timing.
Z||000009||David||08-30-99||02:43 PM||||The few times that I have seen a red hot manifold, the ignition timing has been retarded.
Z||000010||Richard Parnell||09-01-99||06:03 PM||||I have an 87 sc 4 cyl w/DIS.  If the oxygen sensor is disconnected the engine will run hot enough to make the manifold glow red.  Mine was so hot I could see right through it.
Z||000011||difranco||09-03-99||05:20 AM||||Found a vacuum leak in the cold start valve hose which connects the cold start valve to the intake.  Replaced hose no more problems.  Timing was a little off not much.  Thanks for all the help.
Z||000012||mwbackus||09-03-99||11:57 AM||||You are welcome.  Its nice to know that the permanently embeded grease under my finger nails has served some purpose. :-)<p>Mike
Z||000013||Steven||09-04-99||10:47 PM||||I had that problem once. As it turned out the pipe connecting the Plenum to the EGR valve had a crack up at the flange that mounts it to the plenum. Now when this was happing I was also get a check engine light. It's worth looking at. 
